feat(ui): editorial notice on reprinted government feeds (Cyrus chose option C)

The ticker and the breaking-news panel run gov_news_law_order -- official
law-and-order releases reprinted verbatim. That is a defensible source, but a
reader is entitled to know they are looking at government copy rather than at
this project's own reporting, so the panel now says so.

The notice lives in panels.json as a `disclaimer` field, not in the renderer:
the wording is an editorial decision, and keeping it in config means it is
reviewable in a diff and changeable without touching code. It renders above the
footer, deliberately visible rather than hidden in a tooltip -- a disclaimer
nobody reads is not a disclaimer.

The validator now checks it: bilingual, and at least 20 characters per language.
A presence-only check would accept a one-word notice that tells the reader
nothing, which is the failure worth blocking. Negative-tested by feeding it a
2-character notice and confirming the build fails.

Also verified the Worker's actual SSRF defences against a running instance
rather than by reading the code -- 14 probe cases, all refused correctly:
non-registry host, evil-TLD lookalike, suffix trick, userinfo trick, plain
http, credentials in URL, file scheme, cloud metadata endpoint (169.254.169.254),
localhost, RFC1918, and a non-standard port on an allowed host. The whitelist is
exact-match with no wildcards.

The aircraft check no longer reports a red failure for calm airspace: the sky
over Hong Kong is occasionally genuinely empty, so it retries once against a
cleared cache and, if still empty, requires the layer to be correctly wired.
A misconfigured layer still fails. Harness 63 -> 64 checks.
